=3Ddpl_CEjisbLnWxFQGUTRjiHGoKY1oV5c"></div></figure>
<p>So, whenever a specific sequence of four numbers occurs at the beginning=
 of a row, it will generate a predictable sequence of four numbers in the f=
ollowing row. Extending this observation to the entire pattern, we can conc=
lude that since the pattern repeated once (from rows 3 to 6 to rows 7 to 10=
), it will continue to repeat indefinitely.</p>
<p>This gives us the below cyclic rationale:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>If <code>n</code> is odd</strong> (<code>n % 2 !=3D 0</code>), =
return 2.</li>
<li><strong>If <code>n</code> is a multiple of 4</strong> (<code>n % 4 =3D=
=3D 0</code>), return 3.</li>
<li><strong>Else</strong>, return 4.</li>
</ul>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display:flex;justify-content:center"><=
